data language_target_registry_totality_law: String = "NO INVISIBLE REMAINDER (operator requirement C, 2026-08-04). This registry may not read as complete because two specimen rows exist. Every target-bearing surface this repository currently carries is EITHER joined to a registered subject OR appears here as an exact row with no identity, which the join kernel then reports as SurfaceMissingTargetIdentity. A surface that is simply absent from these lists is the one state the wall cannot see, which is why the population definition below is declared rather than implied, and why the TargetVocabulary rows carry an explicit scaffold note rather than a silent transcription. The registry now registers 26 subjects, so most rows below ARE identified; the ones that are not — machine_code, the two runtime checkers, the legacy frontier table — each carry a note stating why joining them to a subject would be wrong rather than merely undone. Read those notes before treating an unidentified row as outstanding work: two of the four are deliberate and correct."

data language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note: String = "DECLARED SCAFFOLD, with its dissolution trigger — the TargetVocabulary rows below are HAND-DECLARED, and they were meant to be derived. The intent was to read v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment's target_ast_vocab_modules and target_ast_vocab_module_prefixes directly, so a module added to the lens would appear here automatically as an unidentified surface and the second-hardcoded-roster fork would close by construction. THAT IMPORT DOES NOT COMPILE TODAY, and the blocker is not in this module: the lens fails to resolve fold_list and list_snoc_item from v2.std.algebra even when compiled from its OWN source root as the entry — measured 2026-08-04. That is the Class B pool-membership defect DESIGN's import-strip open thread describes: a module's bare cross-module references resolve only when some unrelated import elsewhere in the assembled closure has already dragged the target into the pool, so the lens resolves inside the whole-corpus compile and not in a narrow closure. Transcribing the two lists here would be the workaround DESIGN section 5 forbids — an unmarked second representation of the very roster this registry exists to unfork — so they are declared as a counted scaffold instead. DISSOLVE-ON: the lens resolves under a narrow closure (Class B fix), at which point these rows become the fold over its lists; and ultimately the reverse direction, the lens deriving its confined set FROM this registry, which is the obl-lang-support-registry to obl-lang-no-new-debt-gate prerequisite edge and is not done here."

data language_target_registry_population_definition: String = "Target-bearing surfaces enumerated from six declared authorities: (1) StdLanguageCatalogue — std.languages Language rows; (2) TargetModelDeclaration — committed TargetModel-bearing language modules; (3) EmitHostExecution — tools.emit_host_transport per-language smoke fns; (4) TargetVocabulary — the confined module list and prefix list v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment enforces; (5) RuntimeChecker — external toolchains a support claim would execute against; (6) LegacyFrontier — gunbc.plans.language_target_self_host_frontier's prose rows. ALL SIX authorities are HAND-DECLARED at this revision — authority 4 was intended to be derived and is a counted scaffold with a stated trigger (language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note); nothing in this module is derived from the live tree."

data language_target_registry_maximum_conclusion: String = "MAXIMUM CONCLUSION — what a green totality check here does and does not establish. IT ESTABLISHES: every surface in the declared population above is accounted for, each either joined to a registered subject or reported as an exact unidentified row; IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH that the declared population is COMPLETE with respect to the repository. All six authorities are hand-declared, so a target-bearing surface nobody wrote a row for is invisible to every assertion over this module — the failure mode loyal-ant-382 measured at 43 percent under-selection on a text-keyed census and this session reproduced at 100 percent on the ShellCommand sink. Closing that needs a Node-tree observer deriving the population from the real module tree, which is the residue-census obligation and is NOT built here. No count in this module may be quoted as a corpus claim."

data registry_revision_note: String = "WHAT THE REVISION ANCHORS, STATED PRECISELY BECAUSE THIS MODULE HOLDS TWO KINDS OF ROW AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS A MEASUREMENT (corrected after review 48113, which correctly caught the anchor reading as though it covered everything here). The SURFACE CENSUS is an observation of a tree: someone walked six declared authorities and wrote down which target-bearing surfaces existed. That is a measurement, it goes stale silently, and subject_revision names the commit it was enumerated against — a row set carried without its revision is a measurement pretending to be a standing fact (DESIGN section 5's oracle rule). The REGISTRATIONS and the identity JOINS are not measurements at all: a registration is grounded in a cited upstream authority, and a join says which subject a surface belongs to, which is a fact about the subject rather than about the tree on a given day. Neither goes stale when the tree moves, so neither carries a revision. THE ANCHOR THEREFORE STAYS AT THE COMMIT THE POPULATION WAS ENUMERATED AGAINST, and the D0 change that registered 26 subjects did not advance it because it added no census row and removed none — it changed which subject the existing rows join, and rewrote every note that the new joins made false. Advancing the anchor for an authoring change would have been the worse error: it would assert the population was re-walked when it was not."

data language_target_registry_subject_revision: String = "1d24bf80699"

data subject_ref_derivation_note: String = "EVERY SUBJECT REF BELOW IS A PROJECTION OF ITS SCOPE, never re-authored beside it. An earlier revision declared gnu_bash_subject_ref as a hand-written DeclarationRef repeating the module path and decl name the scope already carries — two writable representations of one identity, which can disagree exactly the way target_grounding_law describes. Reading scope.subject instead makes the disagreeing state unrepresentable: there is only one place the subject is stated, and it is the carrier that also holds the citations. Verified by execution 2026-08-04 that a top-level data declaration may project a field of an imported scope, so this costs nothing."

data gnu_bash_subject_ref: ExternalSubjectRef = gnu_bash_model_scope.subject

data d0_subject_registration_note: String = "D0 IS THE ROWS, AND THIS IS WHAT REPLACED THE BLOCKER NOTE THAT STOOD HERE. The previous revision recorded that registering TypeScript, C, C++, Verilog, SPICE, Python, Go, Java, Kotlin, Swift, LLVM IR, WebAssembly, PTX, machine code, Lean, SQL, HTML, CSS, ECMAScript, JSON, YAML and Markdown was impossible because the construction wall requires a TargetGrounding and NONE of them had a cited subject declaration — Bash was the only one, and it had only just been authored. Twenty-three cited subject carriers now exist under dag/extdeps/languages/*/subject.dag, each enrolled in scope_carrier_paths, and the registry registers 26 targets: 24 external plus the two project-grounded ones. THE CITATION RULE THIS BATCH FOLLOWED, because it is the whole value of the rows: every locator was FETCHED LIVE on 2026-08-04 and its title and publisher confirmed from the response before it was written down. None was recalled. Where an upstream could not be read, the target was NOT registered and the cause was recorded in unregistered_target_findings — three of them — because a plausible URL for an unverified authority would make the wall green while grounding it in nothing, which is worse than the honest absence the blocker note described."

data unregistered_target_finding_note: String = "TYPED NON-REGISTRATIONS — the residue of verify-or-refuse, carried as rows so it is COUNTED and prioritizable rather than absorbed into silence (the extdeps.pricing.object_storage RateUnavailable precedent, where vendors whose rates were unreadable landed as typed unavailability instead of being filled from memory). Each row names a target that a reader would reasonably expect in the registry, and the exact reason it is absent. Two distinct causes appear and they need different work: an unreadable upstream authority is fixed by obtaining the document, while a fused surface is fixed by decomposing the surface — no amount of citation work registers x86-64 while machine_code still fuses three ISAs into one coproduct. These are NOT parked targets: a parked target is registered and grounded, these are ungrounded and therefore unwritable as registrations by construction."

type TargetNonRegistrationCause
  = UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable { attempted_locator: String, observed: String }
  | SurfaceFusesIndependentSubjects { surface_module: String, fused_subjects: String }

type UnregisteredTargetFinding {
  target_label: String
  cause: TargetNonRegistrationCause
}

data unregistered_target_findings: List<UnregisteredTargetFinding> = [
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "SQL (ISO/IEC 9075)",
    cause: UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable {
      attempted_locator: "www.iso.org/standard/76583.html",
      observed: "HTTP 403 Forbidden — the ISO catalogue refuses this fetch, so neither the standard designation nor its title could be confirmed from the source. Note the contrast with C and C++, which ARE registered: their ISO standards are equally unreadable at iso.org, but their working groups WG14 and WG21 publish readable committee pages that state the designation, and SQL's SC32/WG3 has no equivalent readable page that was found. NEXT TRIGGER: any readable authority that states the ISO/IEC 9075 designation and title. Separately, this repository's SQL surfaces carry a SqlDialect coproduct over PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQLite, so even once the standard is cited, the dialects are independently governed implementations needing their own subjects."
    }
  },
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "x86-64",
    cause: UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable {
      attempted_locator: "www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html",
      observed: "HTTP 403 Forbidden from Intel; the AMD64 Architecture Programmer's Manual at amd.com timed out after 60s. x86-64 is additionally DUAL-GOVERNED — Intel and AMD each publish their own architecture manuals for it — so registering it needs a decision about whether that is one subject with two citations or two subjects, which cannot be made without reading either document. NEXT TRIGGER: either vendor manual becomes readable."
    }
  },
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "Aarch64",
    cause: UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable {
      attempted_locator: "developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0487/latest/",
      observed: "The Arm developer site redirects to support.arm.com and renders its documentation through JavaScript; the fetch returned only the page chrome ('Documentation - Arm Developer') with no title, document number, or architecture profile readable. The document number DDI 0487 is widely known, but writing it here from memory is exactly the recalled citation this batch refuses. NEXT TRIGGER: a readable Arm Architecture Reference Manual locator."
    }
  },
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "machine code (the surface, not an ISA)",
    cause: SurfaceFusesIndependentSubjects {
      surface_module: "v2.extdeps.languages.machine_code",
      fused_subjects: "X86_64Isa, Aarch64Isa, RiscV64Isa — three independently governed upstreams in one Isa coproduct. See machine_code_fusion_note. RISC-V is separately registered as its own subject because its authority verified; this row records that the SURFACE still cannot join one identity, which is a decomposition obligation and not a citation one."
    }
  }
]

fn unregistered_target_finding_count() -> Int {
  fold(unregistered_target_findings, init: 0, f: fn(acc, r) { acc + 1 })
}

data project_grounded_targets_note: String = "The two PROJECT-grounded targets, and they are here for a reason beyond being cheap: ProjectGrounding had zero inhabitants, so an entire arm of the grounding coproduct was unexercised. These are also the two rows that could not have existed under a universal ExternalSubjectRef — the .dag language and the controlled-English medium are this project's OWN targets, with no independently governed upstream to cite, and forcing them through an external scope would have made them claim a governance they do not have. Their authority declarations are real and symbolic: dag_item_forms is the .dag surface-form authority, FormalSentence the controlled-English one. Neither carries an extdeps_model_scope and neither should."

